Two design notes: digitizing somebody's tapes, and signing in with the passkeys we already have

From the same spoken briefing as #71, but these are the *services* half, so they
go where this repository already keeps work that is "worked far enough to be
resumed, but deliberately not built" — `DESIGN-NOTES.md`. Nothing is built.

## The correction worth protecting

You caught yourself mid-sentence and it is the most useful thing in the
briefing:

> There was a contact sheet. That's what I'm — oh my god — that I've been
> telling you proofing this entire time. Contact sheet is the better inspiration
> example.

`.proofing-engine` today is about **AI generation** — sweeping what a generator
made and deciding which outputs are any good. FCPM is not doing AI-gen capture.
What FCPM wants is the other thing in that repository: crawling remote videos,
**requesting byte ranges**, decoding frames at an interval, and a grid where the
interval is something you change by pointing at part of the timeline. Not a
transcode and not a download.

I checked, and **proofing already has half of it** — the sweep view renders a
grid where a video tile has no controls because *"the pointer is the playhead"*,
seeking to whatever fraction of the duration you hover. What it does not have is
interval extraction over byte ranges from a remote file, a zoomable interval, or
kept frames. The note says that should land in proofing rather than in a fork
here, per *"I don't wanna be editing it only for FCPM."*

## The permission model is the good idea

> What we don't wanna do is have them give us edit permission to their folder
> sources. So what I think should happen is that they could give us a folder
> with a shortcut to their stuff inside of it in Google Drive itself. That way we
> can read it, but **by construction** we don't have the ability to edit it.

A Drive shortcut inherits its target's permissions, not the containing folder's,
so a customer can drop a shortcut to their footage into a folder shared with us
and we get the bytes without any grant that could destroy the original. **The
safety is structural, not procedural** — nobody has to remember not to write,
because there is nothing to write with.

That is the same argument the Wix standing order makes from the other direction,
and the same one #70 makes about internal documents: an arrangement where the
mistake is impossible beats one where it is merely forbidden. Three instances is
a house style, so the note says so.

## Also recorded

- **Start with a script.** *"We don't need an AI worker yet."* The AI worker is
  a later shape of the same pipe, not a prerequisite. stagecraft has proven the
  cooperative-GPU end is available but is explicitly not required here.
- **Two intakes, one pipe.** A customer's Drive link and a tape captured in the
  studio differ only in where the file starts life. VHS is real-time and cannot
  be engineered around, which is what makes *"our station getting video
  constantly spit out of it"* the normal state rather than a burst.
- **A library holding does not have to be bytes in git.** First case in this
  repository where the library is asked to know about something it deliberately
  is not carrying.
- **The drive is being partitioned now** — eight boxes, under four gigabytes
  each, different uses, coordinated. Small enough that *where a capture goes* is
  a real decision rather than a default, which is probably why it is happening
  before the pipe exists.
- **The one AI-gen-shaped thing that transfers is routing, not capture**: a
  private browser extension that shuttles a downloaded stock asset to the right
  partition for the box you are sitting at.

## Workstation sign-in is nearer than it sounds

Today it is one shared OS account with a PIN, and identity is whichever browser
profile you clicked — *"it's clear that no one has tackled it yet."*

The mechanism you want comes from the part of ablative that is **not** coming
over — the shroud's QR-on-screen, get-in-with-your-phone idea, *"without
accounts on the device."* What the note adds is that every piece already exists
in this repository, built for other reasons: passkey enrolment and assertion in
the broker, signed claims in `mint-claim.py`, a QR standing in for a session at
`/check-in/`, and identity policy in `_data/authorize.yml`. **A workstation
sign-in is check-in with a different consequence**, which suggests a new consumer
of the broker rather than a new system beside it.

And the constraint that shapes it is an accessibility constraint, so it is
written as one:

> Not everyone wants to pull their phone out, especially if they're old. So
> there are gonna be multiple ways to do this stuff.

Phone-as-key is one door, never the only door. The failure being prevented is an
older member standing in front of a machine that will not let them work until
they produce a smartphone.

**One flagged collision:** `_data/authorize.yml` currently calls claim-link
forwarding deliberate and allowed. A link that opens a workstation is exactly
the case `RESERVE-DESIGN.md` already flagged as needing one-to-one claims. The
note says to read the two together rather than resolving it, because resolving
it is a policy decision.

## A merge note

These notes cite `_data/authorize.yml`, `_data/identity.yml` and
`_data/checkin.yml` by their paths on `main` today. **#70 moves those to
`site/_data/`.** No merge conflict — this appends in a region #70 does not touch
— but if #70 lands first, four path references in the new sections want a
`site/` prefix. Small and mechanical; I can do it as a follow-up or you can take
it with the merge.
